  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 403 PM EDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis:\n\nTroughing will continue to slide to our east through the end of the \nweek as ridging folds over Ontario and the Great Lakes. Surface high \npressure will continue to build across the region, becoming centered \nmore squarely overhead tonight into Friday. The pattern will amplify \nthrough the weekend as upstream troughing punches overhead, \nsupporting a cyclone well to our north that looks to swing an \nattendant cold front through the area in the late Saturday/early \nSunday timeframe. High pressure then looks to build back into the \nGreat Lakes on the backside of this system to start next week before \ntroughing looks to settle in by the middle of next week.\n\nForecast Discussion:\n\nRain-free weather is expected to persist through the end of this \nweek and into this weekend. With mostly clear skies and calm winds \nin store tonight, efficient radiational cooling will allow for \ntemperatures to quickly drop -- potentially dipping into the upper \n30s and low 40s across the typical coldest low-lying locations of \ninterior northern Michigan. Temperatures ranging from the mid 40s to \nlow 50s are anticipated elsewhere. Afternoon temperatures then look \nto warm back into the mid 70s to low 80s for most areas Friday and \nSaturday. \n\nOur next round of rain chances return late Saturday as the \naforementioned front swings through the area. With a dry airmass \noriginally in place ahead of the front in tandem with the \nsystem being far to our north, the relatively moisture-starved \nenvironment looks to keep coverage to isolated/scattered -- with\nthe potential that little/no rain develops along/ahead of the \nfront and many areas of the Northwoods stay dry through the \nweekend. High pressure building in will likely keep dry weather \nin the forecast Sunday and Monday with our next chances \npotentially holding off until closer to the middle of next week.",
